    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season didn't merely end - it sputtered out in a long, ugly skid that left Toronto staring at a 32-36-14 record, eighth in the Atlantic Division, and 78 points in the ledger. That's not a bad bounce or a cruel whistle; that's a team that spent the spring punching holes in its own floor. <strong>L7</strong> tells the story better than any polished exit interview ever could. The Maple Leafs were never close enough to smell postseason oxygen, and the 31-game gap behind the division pace underlines how far the climb truly was. Now comes the hard part, the part Toronto has postponed too many times: sorting the roster, the bench, and the front office with a colder eye than sentiment allows. The cap will bite. The room will change. Some familiar names will be shopped, some will be spared, and every decision has to answer the same old question - who can actually drag this club out of the middle? <strong>What matters next is not a moral victory tour but a ruthless summer reset, with coaching scrutiny, roster turnover, and draft planning all on the table.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 18, 2026"
      },
